<div class="article-body">

<p>The short answer: change the water in a pumped or aerated hydroponic system about every 2 weeks, and never at all in a Kratky jar. But that answer alone will cost you money, because the number that actually decides your schedule is not days. It is gallons per plant.</p>

<p>I know because I changed my water every Sunday for four months, like a religion. Two gallons of fresh nutrients, out the door, every week. By the time I did the math, I had poured roughly 20 gallons of nutrient solution down the kitchen sink. About $30 of nutrients, for lettuce that looked exactly the same as when I started.</p>

<p>Then I read an extension sheet that said 2 to 4 weeks, a forum post that swore by 7 days, and one guy who claimed he had not changed his reservoir in 11 weeks. All three were right. None of them mentioned the variable that decides it.</p>

<h2 id="number">1. The Number That Actually Decides It (Not Days)</h2>

<p>Hydroponic water change frequency is a function of volume, not time. Specifically, how much water sits in the reservoir for each plant drinking from it.</p>

<p>A big leafy plant in late veg drinks about a gallon of water a week. Put that plant in a quart mason jar and the entire reservoir is gone in about 4 days. Put the same plant in a 5-gallon bucket and you are topping off 20% of the volume each week. Same plant, same nutrients, two completely different schedules. That is why two people can argue about this for an hour and both be correct.</p>

<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> small reservoirs drift fast and big reservoirs drift slow. A jar needs attention every few days. A bucket every 10 to 14 days. A 20-gallon tote can go three weeks in cool weather.</p>

