<p>If you only remember one row, remember this one: the smaller the reservoir, the shorter the leash. A quart jar of basil is not a small version of a 5-gallon bucket. It is a system that drifts five times faster.</p>

<h2 id="why">2. Why Water "Goes Bad" (It Is Not the Water)</h2>

<p>Water does not spoil like milk. Nutrient solution goes out of balance, and it does it in three ways.</p>

<p><strong>Evaporation concentrates everything.</strong> When water leaves the reservoir as vapor, the dissolved minerals stay behind. Add nothing and the solution still gets stronger. This is the one that catches beginners, because the water level looks fine while the concentration climbs past what your roots can handle. That is also how you get the burnt leaf tips in my <a href="/posts/hydroponic-leaves-turning-yellow">yellow leaves guide</a>: the plant was never over-fed on purpose. It was over-fed by evaporation.</p>

<p><strong>Plants do not drink water and nutrients in equal ratios.</strong> In fast growth, some plants pull water faster than salts, so the solution gets stronger. In other stages they pull certain salts faster than water, and the reading drops. Either way, the recipe you mixed on day one is not the recipe that is circulating on day nine.</p>

<p><strong>Life gets in.</strong> Roots shed cells. Algae finds any light that leaks into the reservoir. Bacteria settle on every surface once the water warms up. None of that is visible in a clear bucket until you notice the smell. My <a href="/posts/how-to-clean-hydroponic-algae">algae cleanup guide</a> covers the light-blocking part of this, and my <a href="/posts/hydroponic-root-rot-fix">root rot guide</a> covers what happens when the warm, still water finally wins.</p>

<p><strong>Here is why that matters:</strong> a water change is not about replacing dirty water. It is about resetting a ratio. Once you understand that, the whole schedule becomes obvious, and it stops being about Sundays.</p>

<h2 id="topoff">3. The Top-Off Rule I Use Now</h2>

<p>Instead of the calendar, I use the volume of water I have added back.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">The Part Nobody Tells You: The One-Third Rule</div>
  <p>Track how much water you pour back into the reservoir. When the total you have topped off adds up to about one third of the reservoir's original volume, stop guessing and do a full change. At that point the mineral ratios have drifted too far for the readings to mean anything useful.</p>
</div>

<p>In a 5-gallon bucket, that is about 1.6 gallons of top-offs. For my lettuce, that lands right around 12 days. For a quart jar, it is a cup and a bit, which lands at about 4 days. The rule scales itself.</p>

<p>Two more things I do at every top-off, because they cost nothing.</p>

<p><strong>Top off with weak nutrient water, not plain tap water.</strong> If you only ever add plain water, you dilute the salts the plant has not finished with. A half-strength mix keeps the ratio closer to where it started. In flower or heavy fruiting, I keep it closer to full strength.</p>

<p><strong>Leave the tap water out overnight.</strong> Municipal water carries a chlorine residual, and most of it off-gasses in about 24 hours. Note that chloramine does not, so if your water utility uses chloramine you need a dechlorinator instead of patience. Either way, water closer to room temperature is easier on warm roots than cold water straight from the faucet.</p>

<p>And if your tap water is hard, you have a second reason to stop changing weekly. Hard water carries carbonate alkalinity, which holds pH up against your acid. Fresh tap every week means you fight that buffer from scratch every week, and the pH never settles. Testing your source water once, before you blame your nutrients, saves months of confusion. My <a href="/posts/hydroponic-nutrient-guide">nutrient guide</a> walks through the mixing order if you want the full recipe.</p>

<h2 id="signs">4. Five Signs That Beat Any Calendar</h2>

<p>These are the signals I check before I change anything. Any two of them together and I change the reservoir the same day, schedule be damned.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>The reading climbs while the water level drops.</strong> Stronger solution with less water is the signature of evaporation, not of hungry plants.</li>
  <li><strong>pH will not stay put.</strong> If you correct it and it slides back within hours, something alive is working in there. That is a change-and-scrub, not a dose.</li>
  <li><strong>It looks or smells wrong.</strong> Cloudy, brown, or a sour tang means bacteria or algae is winning. Do not taste it. Do not wait for the weekend either.</li>
  <li><strong>Roots go brown and slick.</strong> Healthy hydroponic roots are white and a little fuzzy. Brown and slimy means the environment needs a reset, not another additive.</li>
  <li><strong>You passed the one-third mark.</strong> This is the quiet one, and it is the reason I keep a scrap of paper taped to the bucket with tick marks on it.</li>
</ul>

<p>Pay attention to this number: 70F. That is where dissolved oxygen in a reservoir starts dropping fast enough to matter, and it is the reason warm water and rot show up together. A grow light running close to the bucket lid heats the water from above while you are staring at the leaves. If your reservoir is running warm, that is a <a href="https://tabletopharvest.com/grow-lights" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">light placement problem</a> as much as a water problem. Raising the light a few inches costs nothing and solves both.</p>

<h2 id="toooften">5. Can You Change It Too Often? Yes</h2>

<p>This is where the sources start disagreeing, and both sides are right.</p>

<p>Every grow shop guide says change weekly. The university extension sheets say every 2 to 4 weeks for small home systems. That looks like a contradiction until you notice the water volumes they assume. The shops are talking to people running multi-gallon, high-demand systems. The extension sheets are talking to someone with a lettuce bucket on a windowsill.</p>

<p>But there is a real downside to over-changing, and I found it the hard way. I dumped a full-strength, hours-old reservoir three days after mixing it, because a forum said to. The fresh water went in cold. My lettuce stalled for three days, the new growth went pale, and the roots sulked. Nothing was wrong with the plants. I had just reset the environment they had spent days acclimating to, and I did it out of habit.</p>

<p>So here is the honest part: changing more often is not the safe default. Changing on signal is. You cannot shock a plant with a water change it actually needed.</p>

<h2 id="kratky">6. Why a Kratky Jar Needs Zero Changes</h2>

<p>If you started with the <a href="/posts/kratky-method-for-beginners">Kratky method</a>, you can close this tab. The whole design is one fill, one crop.</p>

<p>You fill the jar once at the start. As the plant drinks, the level drops, an air gap forms under the net cup, and the roots above the waterline pull oxygen straight from the air. If you "help" by topping it back up, you drown the air roots and the plant wilts in a system that has no pump to blame. And if you dump the jar to refresh it, you throw away the layered root zones the plant built for itself.</p>

<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> in Kratky, the falling water level is the system working, not a problem to solve. Match the reservoir size to the crop so one fill lasts one full cycle, and you never change water at all. Two exceptions: a small jar in a very hot kitchen can run low before harvest, and a big kale plant in a small jar will drink the reservoir dry. In both cases the fix is a bigger container next time, not a mid-cycle refill.</p>

<h2 id="choose">7. Three Ways to Handle This</h2>

<p>You do not need much to get this right. Here is the honest spread of options.</p>

<p><strong>Option A: the calendar only.</strong> Change every 2 weeks and hope. It works in a 5-gallon bucket with one lettuce. It fails in a quart jar, because two weeks is longer than the jar lasts. Free, and fine if your reservoir is big.</p>

<p><strong>Option B: the meter route.</strong> This is what I actually use, and it is the middle ground. A digital pH meter tells you if the solution is still usable. An EC or TDS meter tells you whether the concentration is drifting up or down, which is the single strongest signal that a change is due. Two small tools, about $25 to $35 total, and you stop guessing entirely.</p>

<p><strong>Option C: the auto-dosing controller.</strong> A few hundred dollars, doses pH and nutrients for you, and logs everything. Genuinely useful if you travel. Overkill if you are growing four heads of lettuce on a kitchen shelf, and one more pump that can fail while you are away.</p>

<p>I run Option B. A budget <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=digital+pH+meter+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">digital pH meter</a> and a cheap <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=EC+TDS+meter+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">EC and TDS meter</a> live in the same drawer as my scissors. Together they cost less than two bottles of nutrients, and they have saved me more than that in one season.</p>

<p>If your reservoir runs warm, the cheapest fix is an <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=aquarium+air+pump+and+air+stone+kit&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">air pump and air stone</a>. Moving water holds more oxygen and resists the stagnant, sour turn that ends in rot. It also makes the reservoir a little more forgiving on the days you are late with the change.</p>

<p>And when a change is genuinely needed, a splash of <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=3+percent+hydrogen+peroxide+food+grade&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">3% hydrogen peroxide</a> in the fresh water knocks back the slime on contact. One teaspoon per gallon is my starting dose. Know what it does, though.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Watch this before you dose peroxide</div>
  <p>Peroxide does not know the difference between bad bacteria and the beneficial microbes in products like Hydroguard. If you run a live reservoir with beneficial bacteria, peroxide undoes that. Pick one strategy per reservoir: sterile with peroxide, or live with beneficials. Never both in the same week.</p>
</div>

<p>If you would rather skip the mixing and just buy a system where the reservoirs are already sized correctly, my <a href="/posts/best-hydroponic-system-for-beginners">starter kit roundup</a> covers the kits I would actually recommend, and the <a href="/posts/hydroponic-growing-medium">mediums comparison</a> covers what to put in the net cups.</p>

<p>One more part that helps if you are on the DWC side of this: a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=5+gallon+DWC+hydroponic+bucket+kit&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">5-gallon DWC bucket kit</a> with a lid and a net pot. Bigger reservoir, longer leash, fewer chances to get the ratio wrong. That is the upgrade I would make before buying anything else.</p>

<h2 id="honest">8. Here Is the Honest Truth</h2>

<p><strong>Here is the honest truth</strong> about the meter route: a budget EC meter drifts, and it needs calibrating with a $10 reference solution every few months or it slowly lies to you. The pH meter has the same problem. That is the real cost of Option B, and nobody mentions it in the product listing.</p>

<p>I still think it is worth it, because a drifted meter is still better than no reading at all, and calibrating takes ninety seconds. But if you will not calibrate anything, buy the cheap liquid <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=pH+test+drops+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">pH test drops</a> instead. They cannot drift, they cost almost nothing, and a color you can still read beats a number you cannot trust.</p>

<p>For my own buckets, the whole routine now fits in one line: top off with half-strength nutrient water, tick a mark on the tape, and do a full change when the marks add up to a third of the bucket. That is about every 12 days. My nutrients last twice as long as they did when I was changing on Sundays, and the lettuce has not noticed a thing.</p>

<p><strong>Your next move:</strong> go measure your reservoir in gallons, count your plants, and write that ratio on a piece of tape. Then stop changing water on a calendar. If you want the tools first, the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=EC+TDS+meter+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">EC and TDS meter</a> is the one I would buy today. It was $16 when I last checked and it bounces between $13 and $20. Anything under $20 is a solid deal.</p>

